.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:435:.docs/plans/storage-shape-review-2026-04-24.md:82:**Implication for DOS-7:** the migration story isn't "build claims table from scratch"; it's "extend the `account_stakeholder_roles` pattern across more claim types, then merge it into the unified `intelligence_claims` substrate." The pattern is proven. Use it as the precedent rather than treating claims as alien.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:439:.docs/plans/storage-shape-review-2026-04-24.md:166:The `entity_graph_version` / `entity_graph_sweep_state` triggers (migrations 113/117/121) are intelligent cache-busting infrastructure. Substrate writes to `intelligence_claims` need to participate in this cache invalidation — otherwise downstream caches miss claim updates.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:440:.docs/plans/storage-shape-review-2026-04-24.md:168:**Mitigation:** DOS-7 schema migration includes adding `intelligence_claims` to the entity_graph_version trigger set. Currently not in DOS-7 scope.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-301.log:441:.docs/plans/storage-shape-review-2026-04-24.md:172:The `account_stakeholder_roles → intelligence_claims` consolidation (DOS-7 §R1.3) writes to a table that's currently the source of truth for stakeholder reads. If the migration runs before reader migration, stakeholder pages break. If reader migration runs before write migration, stakeholder pages double-render.
